<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Commission against Impunity in Guatemala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2007">2007</a>–<a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as CICIG</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Commission against Impunity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> (CICIG) was a United Nations-backed body, set up by agreement with the Guatemalan government, that worked from 2007 to 2019 to investigate criminal networks inside the state. With Guatemalan prosecutors it brought cases against hundreds of officials, and in 2015 its La Línea investigation led to the resignation and arrest of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-perez-molina">Otto Pérez Molina</a>. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-morales">Jimmy Morales</a> refused to renew its mandate.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The treaty-level agreement between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> that set up the commission was signed on 12 December 2006. It made CICIG an independent body to support the Public Prosecutor's Office, the National Civilian Police and other state institutions in sensitive and difficult cases, with the ultimate goal of strengthening Guatemala's own judicial institutions so that they could go on confronting illegal groups and organised crime after it left.</p><p>Its mandate had three aims: to investigate illicit security forces and clandestine organisations, including the links between state officials and organised crime; to support the Attorney General in prosecuting their members and recommend new public policies; and to give technical assistance so that the prosecutors and police would be better equipped once its work ended. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s Code of Criminal Procedure it could join prosecutions as a complementary prosecutor (querellante adhesivo) and bring administrative complaints against officials who obstructed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 24 March 2009 the foreign minister, Haroldo Rodas, asked Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon to extend the mandate for two years, and the extension was confirmed on 15 April 2009; it was renewed again in April 2015. The commission was led by Iván Velásquez Goméz. On 16 July 2015 it published a report on political finance which argued that the financing of Guatemalan politics was illegal by default, since campaigns did not have to disclose where donations came from.</p><p>In 2018 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-morales">Jimmy Morales</a> announced that the mandate would not be renewed, ordered its functions transferred to the Public Ministry and the Ministry of the Interior, and deployed the armed forces near its headquarters. On 7 January 2019 the foreign minister, Sandra Jovel, said the commission had 24 hours to leave, citing alleged illegal acts and abuse of authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> rejected the unilateral termination, and on 9 January 2019 the constitutional court suspended the decision.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Between 2008 and 2019 the commission helped Guatemalan law enforcement dismantle over 70 criminal structures, and a 2022 study estimated that this may have prevented between 20,000 and 30,000 homicides. Its critics included the Central American Socialist Party, which called it an agent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, and others who judged its early cases too lenient.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “International Commission against Impunity in Guatemala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The UN-backed <b>International Commission against Impunity in Guatemala</b> (CICIG), active from 2007 to 2019, became a model for international anti-corruption efforts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
